namespace Eigen {

/** \eigenManualPage TopicStorageOrders Storage Orders, or How the Entries of a Matrix Are Put to Bed in Memory

There are two different storage orders for matrices and two-dimensional arrays, two rival customs between which the whole nation of numerical programmers is divided: column-major and row-major.
This page explains these storage orders and how to specify which one should be used.

\eigenAutoToc


\section TopicStorageOrdersIntro Column-major and row-major storage, the Two Rival Customs

The entries of a matrix form a two-dimensional grid, a handsome square of a place, where every entry has its street and its number. However, when the matrix is stored in memory, which is a single long corridor with doors on one side only, the entries
have to somehow be laid out linearly. There are two main ways to do this, by row and by column.

We say that a matrix is stored in \b row-major order if it is stored row by row; the entire first row being stored first, followed by the entire second row, and so on, like a procession of families walking to church in order of seniority. Consider for example the matrix

\f[
A = \begin{bmatrix}
8 & 2 & 2 & 9 \\
9 & 1 & 4 & 4 \\
3 & 5 & 4 & 5
\end{bmatrix}.
\f]

If this matrix is stored in row-major order, then the entries are laid out in memory as follows, each in his appointed place:

\code 8 2 2 9 9 1 4 4 3 5 4 5 \endcode

On the other hand, a matrix is stored in \b column-major order if it is stored column by column, starting with
the entire first column, followed by the entire second column, and so on, the procession in this case forming up in files rather than ranks. If the above matrix is stored in
column-major order, it is laid out as follows:

\code 8 9 3 2 1 5 2 4 4 9 4 5 \endcode

This example is illustrated by the following Eigen code. It uses the PlainObjectBase::data() function, which
returns a pointer to the memory location of the first entry of the matrix, much as a porter, asked for the first lodger, points at the door.

<table class="example">
